{"timestamp":"2026-04-30T20:57:20.051Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_KEpqArOpe2hGh66RWBM3bDer","process_id":"5074","turn_id":"019de02d-faa0-7443-aa42-bbfbefa3780a","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,180p' /Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-03-19-checkpoints-v2-design.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/entire-migrate-checkpoint-order","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,180p' /Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-03-19-checkpoints-v2-design.md","name":"2026-03-19-checkpoints-v2-design.md","path":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-03-19-checkpoints-v2-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Checkpoints v2: Implementation Status & Remaining Rollout\n\n**Date:** 2026-03-19\n**Updated:** 2026-04-23\n**Status:** Implemented, rollout ongoing\n**Authors:** pfleidi\n**RFD:** [RFD-009 Transcript Compaction](../../../rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md)\n\n## Overview\n\nThis document tracks the current implementation state of checkpoints v2 in the CLI and the remaining rollout work before v2 becomes the only checkpoint format. It replaces the original speculative workstream plan with a state-of-the-world summary grounded in the shipped code under `cmd/entire/cli/`.\n\n**Current state:**\n\n- v2 storage, compaction, migration, read paths, rotation, push logic, and cleanup hooks are implemented in the CLI\n- the rollout is still transitional: v1 dual-write and fallback behavior remain in the codebase\n- asset persistence on `/main` is still deferred\n\nThe implemented v2 design matches the RFD at a high level:\n\n- split storage under `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/`\n- permanent metadata + compact transcripts on `/main`\n- raw transcripts on `/full/current` plus archived generations\n- transcript compaction to Entire Transcript Format at condensation time\n- migration and backfill from v1\n- v1 fallback during rollout\n\nWhat remains is primarily rollout hardening and eventual v1 removal.\n\n## Scope\n\n### In scope\n\n- Document the implemented v2 checkpoint architecture as it exists today\n- Record the current settings and command surface used during rollout\n- Distinguish completed work from rollout tasks that still remain\n- Track the transition from dual-write / fallback behavior to v2-only operation\n\n### Out of scope (deferred)\n\n- Pinning (`entire pin` / `entire unpin`)\n- Cold storage archive (S3/GCS)\n- `retain_raw_logs` compliance mode\n- GitHub Action for automated cleanup\n- Remote migration (`entire migrate --remote`)\n- Persisting extracted transcript assets on `/main`\n\n## Constraints\n\n- The document should reflect actual code and the RFD, not pre-implementation intent.\n- v1 behavior still exists in the CLI and must remain documented until removal is complete.\n- Rollout remains gated by strategy options and migration state in existing repositories.\n- This document is not the normative transcript-format spec; RFD-009 remains the source of truth for storage-format semantics.\n\n## Design\n\n### 1. Strategy Option & Feature Gating\n\nThe current CLI supports both transitional dual-write mode and v2-only mode through `strategy_options` in `settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"strategy_options\": {\n    \"checkpoints_v2\": true,\n    \"push_v2_refs\": false,\n    \"checkpoints_version\": 1,\n    \"full_transcript_generation_retention_days\": 14\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `checkpoints_v2` enables v2 dual-write alongside v1.\n- `push_v2_refs` enables pushing v2 refs during the transition when `checkpoints_v2` is set.\n- `checkpoints_version` is the stronger rollout switch. `checkpoints_version: 2` enables v2 behavior even if `checkpoints_v2` is false, forces v2 ref pushes on, and disables v1 writes in the commit/push path.\n- `full_transcript_generation_retention_days` controls archived `/full/*` generation cleanup and defaults to 14 days when unset or invalid.\n\nThe code path is no longer speculative here: these behaviors are implemented in the `settings` helpers and used by condensation, push, migration, and cleanup flows.\n\n### 2. v2 Ref Structure\n\n```\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main                <- permanent: metadata + compact transcripts\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current        <- active generation: raw logs\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/0000000000001  <- archived generation (deletable)\n```\n\nCustom refs under `refs/entire/` are invisible in GitHub's branch UI, not fetched by default, and not subject to branch protection rules. This is a deliberate choice per the RFD.\n\nThe tree layout within each ref keeps the v1 sharded checkpoint path shape: `<checkpoint_id[:2]>/<checkpoint_id[2:]>/`.\n\n### 3. Checkpoint Store Changes\n\nThe v2 store is implemented as `checkpoint.V2GitStore`. It is separate from the v1 `GitStore`, but composes v1 helper logic for ref-agnostic tree surgery and summary maintenance.\n\nCurrent write split:\n\n- `/main` stores checkpoint metadata, session metadata, `prompt.txt`, `transcript.jsonl`, and `transcript_hash.txt`\n- `/full/current` stores raw transcript content and `raw_transcript_hash.txt`\n- archived `/full/<generation>` refs store older raw transcripts plus `generation.json`\n\nHash placement follows the RFD and the code:\n\n- `transcript_hash.txt` validates compact `transcript.jsonl` on `/main`\n- `raw_transcript_hash.txt` validates raw transcript content on `/full/*`\n\nRead helpers are implemented for:\n\n- reading committed summaries from `/main`\n- reading compact transcript data directly from `/main`\n- reading raw transcript content from `/full/current` with fallback to archived generations\n- falling back to v1 when needed during rollout\n\nTask checkpoint metadata copy is also implemented for v2 writes and migration backfill. Temporary shadow branches remain unchanged and local-only.\n\n### 4. Dual-Write Condensation Path\n\nWhen v2 is enabled, the condensation path already dual-writes:\n\n1. Existing metadata extraction and prompt/transcript capture run as before.\n2. The CLI attempts to compact the transcript to Entire Transcript Format.\n3. The result is written to v2 via `V2GitStore.WriteCommitted`.\n4. In transitional mode, the same checkpoint is still written to v1.\n\nWhen compaction is unavailable, `/main` still gets metadata and prompt content, while raw transcript content remains available on `/full/*`. Explain/read paths then fall back to raw transcript behavior as needed.\n\nStop-time finalization is also implemented. `UpdateCommitted` updates v1 and v2 so mid-turn checkpoints are finalized with:\n\n- refreshed raw transcript content on `/full/current`\n- refreshed compact transcript content on `/main`\n- updated compact transcript hash on `/main`\n\nThe session state now also tracks `compact_transcript_start`, and v2 metadata uses that compact offset rather than the raw-transcript offset when setting `checkpoint_transcript_start`.\n\n### 5. Compaction — Entire Transcript Format\n\nCompaction is implemented in `cmd/entire/cli/transcript/compact`.\n\nImportant current behavior:\n\n- there is no separate header line\n- every emitted line carries `v`, `agent`, and `cli_version`\n- assistant tool results are inlined into tool-use blocks\n- the format is agent-agnostic JSONL intended for downstream readers like `entire explain`\n\nBuilt-in compaction coverage in the current codebase includes:\n\n- Claude Code (JSONL)\n- Gemini CLI (JSON messages array)\n- Cursor (JSONL variant)\n- OpenCode (JSON export)\n- Factory AI Droid (envelope format)\n- Copilot CLI (session format)\n- Codex\n\n### 6. External Agent Compaction Protocol\n\nThe external agent protocol extension is implemented.\n\n- Plugins opt in with `\"compact_transcript\": true` in capabilities.\n- The CLI invokes `compact-transcript --session-ref <path>`.\n- The response carries base64-encoded compact transcript bytes and optional assets.\n\nCurrent limitation: the protocol supports assets, but the CLI does not yet persist extracted assets on `/main`. For now, compact transcripts are persisted, while asset externalization remains deferred.\n\n### 7. Generation Rotation\n\nGeneration rotation is implemented in `checkpoint/v2_generation.go`.\n\nCurrent behavior:\n\n- `/full/current` rotates when the checkpoint count in the generation reaches the hardcoded threshold of 100\n- archived generations use a 13-digit zero-padded numeric suffix\n- `generation.json` is written only when archiving, not during normal `/full/current` writes\n- `generation.json` records only `oldest_checkpoint_at` and `newest_checkpoint_at`\n- concurrent rotation is guarded by re-reading `/full/current` before rotating\n\nThis keeps `/full/current` merge-friendly and makes archived-generation cleanup a ref-deletion problem rather than tree surgery.\n\n### 8. Updated Read Paths\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2667},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-30T20:57:20.051Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_tw7CXMe3EH2dY1g7rLpM9cvF","process_id":"50755","turn_id":"019de02d-faa0-7443-aa42-bbfbefa3780a","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,220p' /Users/pfleidi/entire/company-knowledge/rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/entire-migrate-checkpoint-order","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' /Users/pfleidi/entire/company-knowledge/rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md","name":"rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md","path":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/company-knowledge/rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\ntitle: Transcript Compaction\nstate: draft\nauthor: \"soph\"\ndate: 2026-02-26\nupdated: 2026-03-19\ntags: checkpoints, storage, github-action\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\nSession transcripts on `entire/checkpoints/v1` are 96.5% larger than what the UI needs. We introduce:\n\n1. **Dual-write, with a path to v2-only writes:** The CLI can dual-write raw transcript (`full.jsonl`) and compact **Entire Transcript Format** (`transcript.jsonl`) during rollout, and later switch to v2-only writes once `checkpoints_version: 2` is enabled.\n2. **Split storage:** Permanent data (metadata + compact transcripts) goes on an append-only `/main` ref. Raw logs go on a separate `/full/current` ref that rotates into numbered archived generations when full. All stored under `refs/entire/` — invisible in GitHub's branch UI.\n3. **Cleanup = delete entire generations.** No tree surgery, no force-push, no history rewriting. Just delete the archived ref — all blobs become unreachable.\n\nOnly 2-3 active refs at any time. The CLI stays append-only. The API reads only the compact format.\n\n## Problem\n\nSession transcripts stored on `entire/checkpoints/v1` are significantly larger than what the UI needs. Claude Code's JSONL format includes streaming progress, subagent messages, thinking blocks, full tool output, and per-message metadata that the API parser never reads.\n\nAcross 10 real transcripts (158 MB total), **96.5% of the data is unnecessary for the UI**. Individual files ranged from 63% to 99.9% reduction. Heavy subagent sessions (common with Task tool usage) can be 46 MB raw but only 40 KB after stripping.\n\nAdditionally, transcripts grow over a session — when a user makes multiple commits, each checkpoint stores the full transcript up to that point. The second checkpoint contains everything from the first plus new lines. This duplication is intentional: `entire resume` needs the complete transcript to reconstruct conversation state, and storing the full log at each checkpoint means resume works from any single checkpoint without concatenating prior ones. But after the resume window passes, this duplication is pure waste.\n\n## Proposed Solution\n\n### Dual-write: raw + compact format\n\nRather than only storing agent-native transcripts and stripping them later, the CLI writes **two files** to **two separate refs** at condensation time:\n\n1. **`full.jsonl`** → written to the ephemeral `/full/current` ref. Raw agent transcript (unchanged). Used by `entire resume` to reconstruct conversation state. Deleted with its generation after the retention window.\n2. **`transcript.jsonl`** → written to the permanent `/main` ref. Compact **Entire Transcript Format**. Agent-agnostic, contains only what the UI/API needs. Never deleted.\n\nThis addresses several concerns from the original RFD review:\n\n- **Rome (API) only needs to understand one format** — not Claude, Cursor, Gemini, Codex, and Copilot transcripts individually (Georg's point). Agent-specific parsing stays in the CLI; the API reads only the Entire Transcript Format.\n- **No risky post-hoc transformation** — the compact format is written at recording time by the same CLI version that understands the agent's format. No version skew between writer and compactor.\n- **Cleanup becomes simple deletion** — raw logs live in separate, ephemeral refs. After the retention window, delete the entire ref — no tree surgery, no force-push.\n\n### Entire Transcript Format\n\nA normalized, agent-agnostic JSONL format. One line per conversational turn. Versioned so the API can handle format evolution.\n\nThe implementation does **not** use a separate header line. Instead, every emitted line carries the shared metadata needed by readers:\n\n```jsonl\n{\"v\":1,\"agent\":\"claude-code\",\"cli_version\":\"0.42.0\",\"type\":\"user\",\"ts\":\"...\",\"content\":[{\"id\":\"p1\",\"text\":\"user message text\"}]}\n{\"v\":1,\"agent\":\"claude-code\",\"cli_version\":\"0.42.0\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"ts\":\"...\",\"id\":\"msg_xxx\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"...\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_xxx\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{...},\"result\":{\"output\":\"...\",\"status\":\"...\"}}]}\n```\n\nEvery line carries `\"v\":1`, `\"agent\"`, and `\"cli_version\"` so readers can handle format evolution without requiring a header read first. When we add or change fields, we bump the version. Old readers ignore unknown versions; new readers handle both.\n\n**Entry types:**\n- `user` — user content blocks. The `content` array contains structured blocks rather than a single string. Text is emitted as `{\"id\":\"<prompt-id>\",\"text\":\"...\"}` and image blocks are preserved inline when present.\n- `assistant` — assistant responses with `type`, `ts`, `id`, and `content`. `content` keeps text blocks and `tool_use` blocks.\n\n**Tool result handling:**\n- The implementation does **not** emit a separate `user_tool_result` line type.\n- Tool results are inlined into the preceding assistant `tool_use` block as a `result` object.\n- Tool result output text is preserved in compact transcripts because `entire explain` and related readers …5227 chars truncated…cript-related is written to `/main` for that checkpoint (metadata and prompt are still written as usual). This means:\n\n- Old plugins continue working without changes\n- New plugins opt into compaction when ready\n- **No permanent `full.jsonl` on `/main`** — avoids leaving behind a large, never-compacted file on the permanent ref. If a plugin later adds compaction support, future checkpoints get compact transcripts on `/main` without stale `full.jsonl` files cluttering the append-only history.\n- The CLI/API read path checks for `transcript.jsonl` on `/main` first, then falls back to `full.jsonl` on `/full/current`\n  - This also helps develop the v2 format while working on the compaction mechanism in parallel\n\n**Why base64 encoding**: The `transcript` field contains UTF-8 JSONL bytes that the CLI writes byte-for-byte to `transcript.jsonl` on the `/main` ref. Base64 ensures byte-exact round-tripping — the plugin produces the final JSONL output and the CLI transports it without parsing or re-serialization. This avoids nested JSON escaping (JSONL lines contain quotes, backslashes, and potentially control characters from tool output that would all need escaping inside a JSON string) and is consistent with the encoding used by [`chunk-transcript`](https://docs.entire.io/cli/external-agents#chunk-transcript-max-size-%3Cn%3E). The `assets` array uses base64 for its `data` fields since those carry actual binary content (images, screenshots).\n\n### Git storage model: refs with generational rotation\n\n#### The problem with a single branch\n\nThe current design stores all checkpoints in a single branch (`entire/checkpoints/v1`) under `refs/heads/`. Each `WriteCommitted` creates a new commit on top of the previous one, splicing a new checkpoint subtree into the root tree.\n\nRemoving `full.jsonl` from the latest commit's tree does **not** reclaim space — every prior commit still references the old blob. The only way to free space on a single branch is to squash the entire history into one orphan commit and force-push — with all the downsides that entails (race conditions leading to data corruption, destroyed history, opaque GC timing, reconciliation complexity).\n\n#### Why refs instead of branches\n\nv2 stores everything under `refs/entire/` instead of `refs/heads/`. This is a deliberate choice:\n\n**Invisible in GitHub UI.** Branches (`refs/heads/*`) show up in the branch dropdown, the \"compare & pull request\" banner, and branch protection rules. Checkpoint refs are internal plumbing — they should not clutter the UI or prompt developers to open PRs against them. Custom ref namespaces under `refs/entire/` are invisible in the GitHub branch UI.\n\n**Refs can be pointed elsewhere without force-push.** A ref under `refs/entire/` can be updated to point to any commit — there's no fast-forward enforcement by default. This makes rotation trivial: point `current` to the new commit, point the old name to the archived commit. Branches under `refs/heads/` may have branch protection rules or require fast-forward pushes, adding friction.\n\n**No interference with branch-based workflows.** Branch count limits, CI triggers on branch push, branch cleanup bots, branch protection rules — none of these apply to custom refs. The checkpoint data lives in a parallel namespace that's invisible to standard git tooling unless explicitly accessed.\n\n**Clean fetch semantics.** The default refspec (`+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*`) only fetches branches. Refs under `refs/entire/` are never fetched unless the CLI explicitly requests them. This means `git clone` and `git fetch` never download checkpoint data by default — the CLI fetches exactly what it needs.\n\n**Consistent with v1 migration.** The current `entire/checkpoints/v1` lives under `refs/heads/` (it's a branch). Moving to `refs/entire/` in v2 creates a clean break — the CLI can detect which version is in use by checking the ref namespace.\n\n#### The solution: separate permanent and ephemeral data into different refs\n\nThree types of refs, each with different lifecycles:\n\n```\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main                ← permanent: metadata + compact transcripts\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current        ← active generation: raw logs (rotates when full)\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/0000000000001  ← archived generation (deletable wholesale)\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/0000000000002  ← archived generation (deletable wholesale)\n```\n\n**`/main`** is a single append-only ref with linear commit history. Works like today's `entire/checkpoints/v1` but stores only small, permanent data: `metadata.json`, `transcript.jsonl`, `transcript_hash.txt`, and `prompt.txt`. Deferred features such as persisted `assets/` blobs or pinned raw transcripts can be added later without changing the basic ref layout. `/main` never needs cleanup, grows slowly, and uses standard merge logic for concurrent pushes (same as today — the existing push-and-merge logic in `fetchAndMergeRef` works here).\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2125},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
