{"timestamp":"2026-04-30T22:01:33.096Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_FdgZWQFuBHzAnVyEJm0NwxXF","process_id":"97089","turn_id":"019de066-a5e0-77f2-9c99-3236f9f7f1f1","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n \"compaction|generation|retention|transcript|checkpoint|created_at|migration|metadata\" /Users/pfleidi/entire/company-knowledge/rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/fix/entire-migrate-checkpoint-order","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'compaction|generation|retention|transcript|checkpoint|created_at|migration|metadata' /Users/pfleidi/entire/company-knowledge/rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md","query":"compaction|generation|retention|transcript|checkpoint|created_at|migration|metadata","path":"rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"7:tags: checkpoints, storage, github-action\n12:Session transcripts on `entire/checkpoints/v1` are 96.5% larger than what the UI needs. We introduce:\n14:1. **Dual-write, with a path to v2-only writes:** The CLI can dual-write raw transcript (`full.jsonl`) and compact **Entire Transcript Format** (`transcript.jsonl`) during rollout, and later switch to v2-only writes once `checkpoints_version: 2` is enabled.\n15:2. **Split storage:** Permanent data (metadata + compact transcripts) goes on an append-only `/main` ref. Raw logs go on a separate `/full/current` ref that rotates into numbered archived generations when full. All stored under `refs/entire/` — invisible in GitHub's branch UI.\n16:3. **Cleanup = delete entire generations.** No tree surgery, no force-push, no history rewriting. Just delete the archived ref — all blobs become unreachable.\n22:Session transcripts stored on `entire/checkpoints/v1` are significantly larger than what the UI needs. Claude Code's JSONL format includes streaming progress, subagent messages, thinking blocks, full tool output, and per-message metadata that the API parser never reads.\n24:Across 10 real transcripts (158 MB total), **96.5% of the data is unnecessary for the UI**. Individual files ranged from 63% to 99.9% reduction. Heavy subagent sessions (common with Task tool usage) can be 46 MB raw but only 40 KB after stripping.\n26:Additionally, transcripts grow over a session — when a user makes multiple commits, each checkpoint stores the full transcript up to that point. The second checkpoint contains everything from the first plus new lines. This duplication is intentional: `entire resume` needs the complete transcript to reconstruct conversation state, and storing the full log at each checkpoint means resume works from any single checkpoint without concatenating prior ones. But after the resume window passes, this duplication is pure waste.\n32:Rather than only storing agent-native transcripts and stripping them later, the CLI writes **two files** to **two separate refs** at condensation time:\n34:1. **`full.jsonl`** → written to the ephemeral `/full/current` ref. Raw agent transcript (unchanged). Used by `entire resume` to reconstruct conversation state. Deleted with its generation after the retention window.\n35:2. **`transcript.jsonl`** → written to the permanent `/main` ref. Compact **Entire Transcript Format**. Agent-agnostic, contains only what the UI/API needs. Never deleted.\n39:- **Rome (API) only needs to understand one format** — not Claude, Cursor, Gemini, Codex, and Copilot transcripts individually (Georg's point). Agent-specific parsing stays in the CLI; the API reads only the Entire Transcript Format.\n41:- **Cleanup becomes simple deletion** — raw logs live in separate, ephemeral refs. After the retention window, delete the entire ref — no tree surgery, no force-push.\n47:The implementation does **not** use a separate header line. Instead, every emitted line carries the shared metadata needed by readers:\n63:- Tool result output text is preserved in compact transcripts because `entire explain` and related readers consume it.\n65:**What's excluded (compared to raw agent transcripts):**\n69:- `message.usage` (already aggregated in `metadata.json`)\n71:- Some agent-specific metadata that is not needed by checkpoint readers\n84:The protocol already allows external agents to return extracted binary assets alongside compact transcripts:\n88:  \"transcript\": \"<base64-encoded Entire Transcript Format JSONL>\",\n95:However, the current CLI implementation does **not** persist these assets yet. Built-in compactors also preserve image blocks inline in `transcript.jsonl` rather than externalizing them into an `assets/` tree. Asset externalization is planned for future releases, but is deferred for the initial checkpoints v2 rollout.\n100:refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main → tree:\n102:  ├── metadata.json\n104:  │   ├── metadata.json\n105:  │   ├── transcript.jsonl        # Compact Entire Transcript Format\n106:  │   ├── transcript_hash.txt     # SHA-256 of transcript.jsonl\n114:refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current → tree:\n117:  │   ├── full.jsonl              # Raw agent transcript\n118:  │   └── raw_transcript_hash.txt  # SHA-256 of full.jsonl\n127:Each transcript format has its own SHA-256 hash file, co-located with the data it validates:\n129:- **`raw_transcript_hash.txt`** on `/full/*` — hash of `full.jsonl` (raw transcript)\n130:- **`transcript_hash.txt`** on `/main` — hash of `transcript.jsonl` (compact transcript)\n134:#### Why `raw_transcript_hash.txt` lives on `/full/*`\n136:`raw_transcript_hash.txt` is a hash of `full.jsonl` and lives on `/full/*` alongside it, not on `/main`. This is deliberate:\n138:- **It validates ephemeral data.** The hash is computed from `full.jsonl` — a file that normally only exists on `/full/*` refs unless pinned to `/main`. Storing the hash on `/main` would leave a dangling reference to data …19785 chars truncated…ly would both try to compact old checkpoints. Since stripping logic may differ across CLI versions, this produces divergent blobs and push conflicts. Rejected.\n576:**Separate compacted branch.** Write compacted data to a second branch (e.g., `entire/checkpoints/v2-compacted`), keep `v1` append-only. Doubles branch count, adds read-path complexity in the API (check compacted branch first, fall back to v1). Rejected.\n578:**One branch per user for raw transcripts (Georg's suggestion).** Store raw transcripts on per-user branches for easy pruning without conflicts. Interesting but adds complexity: branch proliferation, discovery logic, and the CLI would need to know the user identity to pick the right branch. The generational `/full/*` refs achieve the same conflict-free cleanup without per-user branches.\n580:**One ref per checkpoint (split refs).** Each checkpoint gets its own ref(s) — `/main` for metadata, `/N/full` for raw logs. Cleanup is elegant (just delete refs), but ref count grows linearly with checkpoint count: 1,000 checkpoints = 2,000+ refs. GitHub recommends staying under 5,000 branches, and every ref is advertised during fetch negotiation. Teams with months of history would hit this limit. Rejected in favor of generational approach that caps refs at ~3.\n582:**Single ref per checkpoint (no split).** Same ref count problem as above, plus cleanup requires tree surgery instead of ref deletion. Rejected.\n584:**Single branch with squash cleanup.** Keep the current `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. Cleanup squashes entire history into one orphan commit and force-pushes. Works but requires force-push (races with concurrent CLI pushes), destroys history, and depends on opaque server-side GC. Rejected in favor of generational approach.\n586:**Server-side compaction in the API.** The API already reads transcripts — it could compact on read. But the API shouldn't write to user repositories, and this couples compaction to API deployment. Rejected.\n590:- **CLI** — dual-write to `/main` (metadata + compact transcript) and `/full/current` (raw logs) under `refs