_hash.txt` lives on `/main`\n145:`transcript_hash.txt` is a hash of `transcript.jsonl` and lives on `/main` alongside it. This enables integrity validation of the compact transcript — the permanent, API-facing format — without depending on ephemeral `/full/*` refs. The CLI writes both `transcript.jsonl` and `transcript_hash.txt` to `/main` at condensation time. The API can optionally use it for cache invalidation or content-addressed lookups.\n149:**New capability**: \"compact_transcript\" declared in the `info` response.\n150:**New subcommand**: `compact-transcript`\n151:**session-ref**: The `--session-ref` argument is the absolute file path to the agent's transcript file.\n153:`entire-agent-<name> compact-transcript --session-ref <path>`\n155:The plugin reads the raw transcript from disk, compacts it into Entire Transcript Format, and outputs JSON to stdout:\n159:  \"transcript\": \"<base64-encoded Entire Transcript Format JSONL>\",\n169:- transcript (required): Base64-encoded Entire Transcript Format JSONL.\n173:If the STDOUT size limit of 10 MB becomes an issue, we could consider implementing a chunked version similar to [`chunk-transcript`](https://docs.entire.io/cli/external-agents#chunk-transcript-max-size-%3Cn%3E).\n177:If the plugin doesn't declare `compact_transcript` capability, the CLI writes `full.jsonl` **only to `/full/current`** — nothing transcript-related is written to `/main` for that checkpoint (metadata and prompt are still written as usual). This means:\n180:- New plugins opt into compaction when ready\n181:- **No permanent `full.jsonl` on `/main`** — avoids leaving behind a large, never-compacted file on the permanent ref. If a plugin later adds compaction support, future checkpoints get compact transcripts on `/main` without stale `full.jsonl` files cluttering the append-only history.\n182:- The CLI/API read path checks for `transcript.jsonl` on `/main` first, then falls back to `full.jsonl` on `/full/current`\n183:  - This also helps develop