isting state, cached values that could be derived\n- **Parameter sprawl** — adding new parameters instead of restructuring\n- **Leaky abstractions** — exposing internal details, breaking existing abstraction boundaries\n- **Stringly-typed code** — using raw strings where constants or typed values already exist in the codebase\n- **Test coverage gaps** — changed code paths not exercised by any test\n\nFor EACH finding: verify the claim by reading the source. Check call sites to confirm the issue is real, not hypothetical.\n\n### Agent 3: Simplification & Efficiency\n\nReview `git diff $BASE` for:\n\n- **Dead code** — unreachable branches, unused functions, struct fields that are never read\n- **Code reuse** — search for existing utilities and helpers that could replace newly written code; flag duplicated functionality\n- **Copy-paste with variation** — near-duplicate blocks that should be unified\n- **Unnecessary abstractions** — wrapper types, indirection, or overly defensive fallbacks that mask errors\n- **Unnecessary work** — redundant computations, repeated file reads, duplicate API calls, N+1 patterns\n- **Missed concurrency** — independent operations run sequentially when they could be parallel\n- **Hot-path bloat** — blocking work added to startup or per-request paths\n- **Unnecessary existence checks** — pre-checking file/resource existence before operating (TOCTOU anti-pattern); operate directly and handle the error\n- **Unnecessary comments** — comments explaining WHAT the code does (well-named identifiers already do that); keep only non-obvious WHY\n\nFor EACH suggestion: verify it does not break existing behavior by checking call sites an…2384 chars truncated…classified as Required, sorted by severity.\n\n### Improvements (follow-up)\n\nNumbered list (continuing the numbering) of findings classified as Improvement. These are presented for awareness but are NOT included in the fix cycle by default.\n\nEnd with a one-paragraph summary: total required vs improvement findings, overall merge-readiness assessment, and any patterns across files.\n\nThen offer two fix modes — these apply to Required findings only:\n\n> **How would you like to proceed?**\n>\n> 1. **Supervised** — I fix one required finding at a time and wait for your approval before the next.\n> 2. **Batched** — I fix all required findings in the order presented and show a combined diff at the end. You can optionally specify a subset (e.g. \"fix 1,3,5-8\") or include specific improvement findings.\n>\n> You can also drop, reorder, or skip any findings.\n\n**STOP and wait for the user's choice.**\n\n## 5. Fix Cycle\n\n### Scope Rules (all modes)\n\n- Make the MINIMAL change that addresses the finding\n- Keep the diff limited to files and lines directly required by the finding\n- If the finding requires a behavior-changing code fix, add or update the directly related test in the same fix step. Prefer writing the failing test first; otherwise add the test immediately after the code change before moving on.\n- Do NOT rename variables, reformat code, or touch lines outside the finding scope\n- Do NOT refactor adjacent code, even if it looks related\n- Do NOT create any git commits — code changes only\n\n### Mode: Supervised\n\nWork through the user's list one at a time:\n\n1. State which finding you are fixing (number and one-line description)\n2. Read the relevant code to confirm the fix approach\n3. Implement the fix — ONLY the code changes for that single finding\n4. Add or update the directly related test in the same diff when the fix changes behavior; if no test is added, state why\n5. **STOP and wait for user approval** before proceeding to the next finding\n\nWhen the user indicates they are done (e.g. \"that's all\", \"done\", \"stop\"), or all findings have been addressed, proceed to Step 6 (Verify Fixes).\n\n### Mode: Batched\n\nFix all Required findings in report order by default. If the user specified a subset (e.g. \"fix 1,3,5-8\"), fix only those — 